# drizzle-kit GENERATED DRAFT OUTPUT — not the migration history.
#
# backend/drizzle.config.ts writes `npx drizzle-kit generate` output here. Two different things
# live under migrations/ and conflating them is a real hazard, so the distinction is worth stating:
#
#   migrations/*.sql       the APPLIED history, single source of truth for the schema
#                          (Principle #6). Tracked on purpose. migration-runner.ts keeps a sha256
#                          content-checksum ledger of these, so every file here is a record of
#                          something that ran against a real database, and editing one is a hard
#                          error rather than a silent divergence from what was applied.
#
#   migrations/drizzle-draft/  machine output nobody has reviewed or run. drizzle-kit diffs the
#                          TypeScript schema and emits whatever the *current* schema implies, which
#                          ignores the hand-written SQL the history contains. Committing it invites
#                          three specific failures: an unreviewed file that looks like an approved
#                          migration, a reviewer mistaking generated SQL for a deliberate change, and
#                          migration:check linting a chain that was never meant to be replayed.
#                          Regenerating is one command, so nothing is lost by ignoring it.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT `migrations/drizzle-draft` WITHOUT A TRAILING SLASH: a bare name would also match a
# *file* of that name, which is not what is being ignored.
migrations/drizzle-draft/

#Ignore vscode AI rules
.github/instructions/codacy.instructions.md
